{"results":[{"url":"https://www.dorsey.com/insights/uk-government-announces-job-support-scheme","final_url":"https://www.dorsey.com/insights/uk-government-announces-job-support-scheme","title":"Insights | UK Government Announces Job Support Scheme","description":null,"language":"en","text":"Materials\nSymposium Agenda\nSessions\n8:30 am - 9:30 am CT\nSession A: The Corporate Compass: Finding True North When Ethical Duties Conflict\nPhilip Kaplan, Senior Legal Counsel, Howden Re\nLucia Suljic, Vice President, Corporate Legal, Canon USA\nNicole Stanton, Partner & Deputy General Counsel, Dorsey & Whitney LLP\nThis panel addresses ethical obligations of in-house counsel, including balancing obligations and duties that may conflict with each other. How should a responsible in-house lawyer deal with those challenging situations? Rather than stick to an academic discussion, this panel will revolve around a series of practical scenarios that are designed to be realistic and timely, including situations where counsel is asked to represent both the company and an individual, how and when to disclose uncomfortable facts and inconvenient documents, issues involving changing firms and companies, and multijurisdictional practice. The panel will feature discussion from two experienced in-house lawyers and Dorsey’s Deputy General Counsel.\nSession B: Managing Rapid Regulatory Change in International Trade and Cross-Border Investment\nNathan Brower, Executive Vice President, General Counsel, Nature’s Sunshine\nGreg Ogunsanya, Senior Vice President, General Counsel and Corporate Secretary, H.B. Fuller\nKathryn Wood, Senior Vice President & General Counsel, DecoPac, Inc.\nDave Townsend and Lawrence Ward, Dorsey & Whitney LLP\nThis program is designed for attorneys advising on cross-border transactions, international trade, regulatory compliance, and corporate governance matters. Panelists will examine recent developments in U.S. trade and investment law and policy, as well as their impact on international collaborations, corporate compliance, and mergers and acquisitions. The discussion will explore how general counsel and in-house legal teams assess and manage evolving trade-related risks within their organizations. Particular focus will be given to developments during the first two years of the second Trump Administration, including changes in tariff policy, tariff refunds, export controls, economic sanctions, and national security reviews of foreign investment. Through a discussion of regulatory trends and practical case studies, attendees will gain a deeper understanding of the legal risks and compliance obligations associated with cross-border business activities, and hear about practical strategies for advising clients and managing compliance obligations in a rapidly changing trade and investment environment.\n9:45 am - 10:45 am CT\nSession A: The Elon Musk Cases: What Tesla and Twitter Can Teach Your Board of Directors\nLoren Unterseher, Managing Partner, Oxbow Industries, LLC\nShulamite White, Chief Legal Officer and Head of People, Motive Technologies\nKirsten Schubert, Tom Swigert, and Jonathan Van Horn, Dorsey & Whitney LLP\nRecent cases involving Elon Musk and his businesses have had significant impact on principles governing board of directors’ fiduciary duties and corporate processes. This panel will examine recent litigation involving Elon Musk, Tesla, and Twitter, and its effects on the law governing the duties of directors and executives, including the executive compensation and director compensation processes, conflicts that may arise when a single executive has control over multiple businesses, and the ramifications of tweets or other public statements by an executive.\nSession B: All Eyes on DEI: Managing Diversity Efforts Amid Regulatory Scrutiny and Potential Litigation\nMaggie Dalton, Senior Commercial Attorney, Cargill; Board Co-Chair, Twin Cities Diversity in Practice\nLaura A. Vannelli, Legal Counsel, Mayo Clinic\nSusan Lorenc and Jack Sullivan, Dorsey & Whitney LLP\nThe legal and regulatory landscape surrounding diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) initiatives has changed dramatically over the past two years. Law firms and legal departments are continuing to grapple with how to recruit, retain, and develop talent from a wide range of personal backgrounds, in a world where previously accepted recruiting and employment practices are facing increased scrutiny and litigation. This program will provide a practical overview of the current legal regime, including recent federal enforcement actions and litigation trends associated with DEI initiatives. Panelists will discuss practical ways in which organizations can pursue and retain legal talent and increase inclusion while minimizing legal, business, and reputational risk.\n11:00 am - 12:00 pm CT\nSession A: Governance in an Age of Increasing Unpredictability\nJames Chosy, Senior Executive Vice President and General Counsel, U.S. Bancorp\nTodd Hartman, Executive Vice President and Chief Legal and Risk Officer, Best Buy Co., Inc.\nAlan Rosselot, Associate General Counsel & Assistant Secretary, Delta Air Lines, Inc.\nCam Hoang and Robert Rosenbaum, Dorsey & Whitney LLP\nMore than ever, ensuring that boards are well-informed and forward-thinking is critical to a company’s success. Join our panel of corporate governance experts as they share their experiences advising boards through crisis and unpredictable events; capitalizing on, but also checking, disruptive technologies; and positioning the company to succeed in an environment of shifting government priorities.\nSession B: A Day in the Life of a Trademark, Copyright & Advertising Attorney: A Hypothetical Case Study\nAlexander Kim, Vice President and General Counsel, Binks\nNate Whitaker, Chief Legal Officer and General Counsel, Crumbl\nCathy Dahl and Tiffany Shimada, Dorsey & Whitney LLP\nWhat issues are companies facing daily while developing rights in intellectual property, protecting those rights, and staying in compliance with myriad potential issues related to advertising, social media, relationships with influencers, and environmental or governmental claims? This panel will feature two in-house counsel who are navigating these issues, utilizing a hypothetical case study, with the support of two Dorsey attorneys helping to develop a roadmap for meeting business objectives and minimizing the risk of claims.\n12:00 pm - 1:15 pm CT\nKeynote Address\nDr. Rebecca Heiss, Stress Physiologist\nDr. Rebecca Heiss is a stress physiologist, author, and sought-after speaker who helps audiences transform fear and stress into fuel for greater performance and resilience. Her research has been recognized as “transformative” by the National Science Foundation, and she is the author of two books, Springboard and Instinct. Through her Fear(less) message, Rebecca inspires audiences with practical, science-based strategies to train our brains to work for us – not against us – and navigate change and uncertainty with greater confidence.","author":null,"published_date":"2020-09-24T00:00:00+00:00","latency_ms":688.8361569999688,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}